\DOC X86_XACCSTEP_TAC

\TYPE {X86_XACCSTEP_TAC : thm * (thm option array) -> term list -> bool -> string -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Symbolically executes one x86 machine instruction, advancing an {eventually}
goal from the current symbolic state to a freshly named successor state and
updating the state-component assumptions.

\DESCRIBE
{X86_XACCSTEP_TAC} takes {th : thm * (thm option array)}, the code/length
theorem paired with a cache of per-instruction decoding theorems, which
identifies the program being stepped through; {excs : term list}, a list of
terms treated as exceptions during the step; {aflag : bool}, controlling
whether the arithmetic/auxiliary-flag effect of the step is kept in precise
form or abstracted into a fresh unconstrained variable; and {s : string}, the
name used for the new state variable introduced by the step.
{
          A ?- eventually x86 P t
   ======================================  X86_XACCSTEP_TAC th excs aflag s
          A' ?- eventually x86 P u
   ```
   Here `u` is the newly named successor state. In `A'`, all assumptions describing readings of the old state (program-code loading, register, flag and memory reads, and the accumulated `MAYCHANGE` relation) are discharged and replaced by corresponding assumptions about the new state, with the `MAYCHANGE` relation extended by the components written by the executed instruction and the instruction-pointer assumption advanced. Assumptions that do not mention a state (side conditions and arithmetic facts) are kept unchanged, and the step adds new arithmetic/defining assumptions `x1 ... xn` (`n` may be zero) relating freshly introduced value variables, such as products, sums and carries, to earlier ones; previously derived value assumptions are retained and the step's own result equation is moved into this non-state block. Depending on `aflag`, flag-related assumptions appear either as explicit symbolic expressions in the old values or as equations against fresh unconstrained variables.
}

\COMMENTS
All observed traces show only this single kind of one-instruction advance, on
goals whose conclusion is an {eventually} statement about an x86 program.

\ENDDOC
